# Normalise line endings to LF in the index AND in the working tree.
#
# Why this file exists. Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, which
# stores LF in the index but writes CRLF into the working tree. That has two costs
# for this repo:
#
#   1. The shell scripts break. A .sh file checked out as CRLF fails under bash
#      with "\r: command not found" -- so a Windows clone could hand someone
#      scripts that do not run, while the copy on GitHub looks perfectly fine.
#
#   2. Comparisons become noise. These skills are maintained as ordinary files in
#      ~/.claude/skills, which git never touches, so they stay LF. Diffing them
#      against a CRLF working tree reports every line of every file as changed.
#      That is not a cosmetic annoyance: it buries the handful of files that are
#      genuinely out of date, and it invites a "fix" that rewrites everything.
#
# eol=lf makes checkout deterministic for everyone who clones, whatever their own
# core.autocrlf happens to be.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never inspected, never converted. text=auto already detects these,
# but stating it means a corrupt PNG can never be blamed on line-ending conversion.
*.png binary
